1
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:08,936
Welcome to LUMAFLOW, a native application for real-time visual effects,

2
00:00:08,936 --> 00:00:14,628
media playback, pixel mapping, spatial lighting effects and show control.

3
00:00:14,628 --> 00:00:20,709
Everything you are about to see is recorded live from the running application.

4
00:00:22,109 --> 00:00:26,502
LUMAFLOW runs completely offline. It needs no account,

5
00:00:26,502 --> 00:00:33,335
no browser and no cloud service. In this recording it renders with a software OpenGL

6
00:00:33,335 --> 00:00:40,086
driver on a virtual display, so frame rates are lower than on a real graphics card.

7
00:00:41,486 --> 00:00:46,273
This is the Live workspace. The effects library is on the left,

8
00:00:46,273 --> 00:00:51,136
with animated thumbnails. In the centre are the preview monitor,

9
00:00:51,136 --> 00:00:57,518
in green, and the program monitor, in red. Show control and the parameter editor are

10
00:00:57,518 --> 00:01:01,165
on the right, and the timeline is at the bottom.

11
00:01:02,565 --> 00:01:07,288
Panels can be docked, tabbed or floated onto another monitor.

12
00:01:07,288 --> 00:01:12,166
Five built-in workspaces arrange the panels for live operation,

13
00:01:12,166 --> 00:01:16,579
programming, mapping, console work and system monitoring.

14
00:01:17,979 --> 00:01:24,230
LUMAFLOW includes thirty-seven GPU effects in five families: particles,

15
00:01:24,230 --> 00:01:29,688
energy, geometry, organic and additional effects such as text,

16
00:01:29,688 --> 00:01:32,769
logos, audio spectrum and feedback.

17
00:01:34,169 --> 00:01:38,225
Selecting an effect loads it on the current layer.

18
00:01:38,225 --> 00:01:43,902
Every effect has speed, scale, rotation, position and colour controls,

19
00:01:43,902 --> 00:01:48,363
plus four effect-specific parameters, such as segments,

20
00:01:48,363 --> 00:01:51,202
zoom and spin for the kaleidoscope.

21
00:01:52,602 --> 00:01:56,731
Here are a few more: smoke, fluid, a Julia fractal,

22
00:01:56,731 --> 00:02:00,535
a wireframe terrain and the energy ring effect.

23
00:02:00,535 --> 00:02:07,578
Shaders are compiled once and cached, so changing parameters never recompiles anything.

24
00:02:08,978 --> 00:02:12,807
The node editor builds new effects without code.

25
00:02:12,807 --> 00:02:19,748
Nodes for coordinates, noise, warping, colour and maths are connected with typed ports,

26
00:02:19,748 --> 00:02:23,498
and the graph is compiled into a single shader.

27
00:02:24,898 --> 00:02:30,893
Invalid connections and cycles are rejected before anything reaches the GPU.

28
00:02:30,893 --> 00:02:35,390
Valid graphs are validated with an isolated test compile,

29
00:02:35,390 --> 00:02:41,859
then shown live on the preview monitor. Saving adds the result to the library as a

30
00:02:41,859 --> 00:02:42,963
custom effect.

31
00:02:44,363 --> 00:02:48,272
Six layers are composited on the GPU with alpha,

32
00:02:48,272 --> 00:02:52,100
additive, multiply, screen and masked blending.

33
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:58,778
Here a particle layer is added over plasma, and a circular mask cuts a window into

34
00:02:58,778 --> 00:02:59,918
the top layer.

35
00:03:01,318 --> 00:03:07,391
Looks can be prepared on the preview composition while the program keeps running.

36
00:03:07,391 --> 00:03:13,315
Pressing TAKE crossfades the preview into the program over the transition time.

37
00:03:14,715 --> 00:03:21,839
A scene stores the complete composition. Scenes can be recalled with their own transition

38
00:03:21,839 --> 00:03:25,521
time, from the show control panel, a cue list,

39
00:03:25,521 --> 00:03:29,203
the timeline, OSC, MIDI or a lighting console.

40
00:03:30,603 --> 00:03:37,539
Video and images play through FFmpeg on background threads with a bounded frame queue,

41
00:03:37,539 --> 00:03:41,652
so disk and decoder activity never block rendering.

42
00:03:41,652 --> 00:03:45,523
Media layers support looping, in and out points,

43
00:03:45,523 --> 00:03:49,959
speed, reverse for short clips, and the same transform,

44
00:03:49,959 --> 00:03:52,862
colour and mask controls as effects.

45
00:03:54,262 --> 00:03:58,240
Text and logo layers render on top of any content.

46
00:03:58,240 --> 00:04:05,243
Missing media files are detected when a project opens and can be relinked from a folder.

47
00:04:06,643 --> 00:04:11,807
The pixel mapper samples the program canvas onto LED strips,

48
00:04:11,807 --> 00:04:16,626
matrices and patched fixtures. In the demonstration rig,

49
00:04:16,626 --> 00:04:23,598
LED tubes, battens, RGBW pars and a sixteen by eight matrix are mapped from their

50
00:04:23,598 --> 00:04:24,889
real positions.

51
00:04:26,289 --> 00:04:32,720
LUMAFLOW handles channel order, gamma, brightness limiting and universe boundaries.

52
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:37,523
Pixels are never split across universes, conflicts are listed,

53
00:04:37,523 --> 00:04:41,939
and only universes you explicitly enable are transmitted.

54
00:04:41,939 --> 00:04:47,130
The identify test pattern shows each element's start and direction.

55
00:04:48,530 --> 00:04:53,054
The 3D stage shows every fixture at its real position,

56
00:04:53,054 --> 00:04:57,075
coloured by the DMX that is actually being sent.

57
00:04:57,075 --> 00:05:03,526
Fixture layouts can be imported from MVR files with their GDTF fixture types.

58
00:05:04,926 --> 00:05:09,494
Spatial effects order fixtures by position: left to right,

59
00:05:09,494 --> 00:05:15,636
centre outward, radial, symmetrical, random with a reproducible seed and more.

60
00:05:15,636 --> 00:05:22,330
Here a chase runs along the blinders, and the washes breathe from the centre outward.

61
00:05:23,730 --> 00:05:27,582
Fixtures with colour but no dimmer use a virtual dimmer,

62
00:05:27,582 --> 00:05:32,672
fixtures with a dimmer get intensity on the dimmer without double dimming,

63
00:05:32,672 --> 00:05:35,836
and intensity-only fixtures receive the level.

64
00:05:37,236 --> 00:05:41,382
The fixture manager holds the patch: fixture types,

65
00:05:41,382 --> 00:05:45,448
modes, universes, addresses, positions and groups.

66
00:05:45,448 --> 00:05:52,521
Capabilities such as dimmer, colour mixing, pan and tilt or multiple cells are detected

67
00:05:52,521 --> 00:05:59,757
automatically from GDTF data, and the patch is checked for overlaps and universe boundary

68
00:05:59,757 --> 00:06:00,326
errors.

69
00:06:01,726 --> 00:06:08,168
LUMAFLOW works as a fixture-style media server for grandMA3 and ChamSys MagicQ.

70
00:06:08,168 --> 00:06:13,875
Both consoles can be connected at the same time on separate addresses,

71
00:06:13,875 --> 00:06:16,322
each with its own mapping set.

72
00:06:17,722 --> 00:06:21,781
No console hardware was available for this recording,

73
00:06:21,781 --> 00:06:28,598
so the following control is a simulated console sending real Art-Net packets to LUMAFLOW.

74
00:06:28,598 --> 00:06:34,956
The master intensity and the layer one effect are being driven over DMX through the

75
00:06:34,956 --> 00:06:35,875
personality.

76
00:06:37,275 --> 00:06:43,531
The fixture personality is generated from the internal parameter registry in four

77
00:06:43,531 --> 00:06:49,787
modes: sixteen, thirty-two, sixty-four and one hundred and twenty-eight channels.

78
00:06:49,787 --> 00:06:53,339
Lower modes are exact prefixes of higher ones,

79
00:06:53,339 --> 00:06:57,510
and channels never move when new parameters are added.

80
00:06:58,910 --> 00:07:05,746
When effects or scenes change, LUMAFLOW proposes an updated profile and shows exactly

81
00:07:05,746 --> 00:07:12,904
what changed. Breaking changes are flagged, nothing is applied without operator approval,

82
00:07:12,904 --> 00:07:19,660
and earlier versions can be rolled back. Export writes a GDTF file for the consoles,

83
00:07:19,660 --> 00:07:21,912
verified by re-importing it.

84
00:07:23,312 --> 00:07:29,729
This is the Standard thirty-two channel mode: master intensity in sixteen bit,

85
00:07:29,729 --> 00:07:35,900
effect selection, opacity, speed, scale, rotation and colour for layer one,

86
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:39,932
scene selection, transition time, a second layer,

87
00:07:39,932 --> 00:07:45,362
strobe and a control channel for go, back, blackout and tap tempo.

88
00:07:46,762 --> 00:07:50,855
The control mapping table connects OSC addresses,

89
00:07:50,855 --> 00:07:55,700
MIDI controls and DMX channels to any parameter or action,

90
00:07:55,700 --> 00:08:02,216
with scaling, inversion, deadband, smoothing, trigger edges and soft takeover.

91
00:08:02,216 --> 00:08:06,560
A learn button creates mappings by moving a control.

92
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:14,673
Ownership policies decide who wins when a console and a touchscreen change the same

93
00:08:14,673 --> 00:08:21,144
value. Feedback is sent back to consoles and controllers without creating loops.

94
00:08:21,144 --> 00:08:25,592
Here OSC messages recall a scene and change the master.

95
00:08:26,992 --> 00:08:32,518
Shows can run from cue lists with manual, auto-follow and wait cues,

96
00:08:32,518 --> 00:08:39,507
or from the multi-track timeline. Automation tracks animate any parameter with curves,

97
00:08:39,507 --> 00:08:43,976
and event tracks trigger scenes, cues and OSC messages.

98
00:08:45,376 --> 00:08:49,833
The timeline can follow its own clock, MIDI time code,

99
00:08:49,833 --> 00:08:56,354
linear time code decoded from audio, or the clock of another LUMAFLOW instance.

100
00:08:56,354 --> 00:08:59,738
The cue list GO button runs the next cue.

101
00:09:01,138 --> 00:09:05,596
Audio analysis splits the input into six frequency bands,

102
00:09:05,596 --> 00:09:11,931
detects beats and estimates the tempo. Here a built-in test signal at one hundred

103
00:09:11,931 --> 00:09:17,719
and twenty-eight beats per minute drives an audio-reactive particle layer.

104
00:09:19,119 --> 00:09:24,308
The program can be sent to multiple displays and LED processors,

105
00:09:24,308 --> 00:09:28,118
with cropping, rotation and scaling per output,

106
00:09:28,118 --> 00:09:31,848
and recorded to video. Calibration sets gamma,

107
00:09:31,848 --> 00:09:38,983
colour balance and white point, and a safety limiter caps flash rates for photosensitive

108
00:09:38,983 --> 00:09:39,794
audiences.

109
00:09:41,194 --> 00:09:45,851
For reliability, LUMAFLOW autosaves, keeps versioned backups,

110
00:09:45,851 --> 00:09:49,975
detects crashes and offers recovery on the next start.

111
00:09:49,975 --> 00:09:56,160
A shader error only affects its own layer, a watchdog monitors the render thread,

112
00:09:56,160 --> 00:10:01,581
and the recovery dashboard lists every fault with a recommended action.

113
00:10:02,981 --> 00:10:09,075
Here is a deliberate fault. A custom effect with a shader error is loaded on layer

114
00:10:09,075 --> 00:10:13,311
two. The compile error is reported in the recovery panel,

115
00:10:13,311 --> 00:10:18,142
but layer one and every output keep running without interruption.

116
00:10:19,542 --> 00:10:23,603
Calibration test patterns help line up projectors,

117
00:10:23,603 --> 00:10:28,395
LED walls and monitors: colour bars with a grid and circle,

118
00:10:28,395 --> 00:10:35,137
a grey ramp for gamma, and full white. The red border marks the edge of the canvas.

119
00:10:36,537 --> 00:10:42,956
Tablets on the local network can control the show through a web interface with token

120
00:10:42,956 --> 00:10:47,618
based roles. Several LUMAFLOW machines can share their state.

121
00:10:47,618 --> 00:10:52,433
Plugins extend the effect library through a C plugin interface,

122
00:10:52,433 --> 00:10:56,483
and the AI assistant turns a sentence into an effect.

123
00:10:57,883 --> 00:11:02,778
Generated effects are validated before they can be activated,

124
00:11:02,778 --> 00:11:09,438
and go to the preview first. The AI assistant works locally by default; an external

125
00:11:09,438 --> 00:11:10,883
model is optional.

126
00:11:12,283 --> 00:11:16,176
On Linux, LUMAFLOW installs from a Debian package.

127
00:11:16,176 --> 00:11:21,938
Windows and macOS installers are produced by the automated build pipeline.

128
00:11:21,938 --> 00:11:25,676
The professional user manual, quick start guide,

129
00:11:25,676 --> 00:11:31,827
console fixture profiles and protocol references are included with the release.

130
00:11:33,227 --> 00:11:39,925
Console integration was demonstrated with simulated control only and must be validated

131
00:11:39,925 --> 00:11:45,377
on real consoles before a production. Thank you for watching LUMAFLOW.
